#import "style.typ": *

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[Prime counts, transforms, and arithmetic tools]

#example[Problem 8. Drawing an integer from Euler's product][Fix a real $sigma>1$ and give $n>=1$ probability $frac(n^(-sigma),zeta(sigma))$. Compute the joint distribution of the exponents of finitely many distinct primes. Use it to recover the mean and variance of $log n$. Justify the passage from finitely many primes to the full logarithm in both moment calculations.]
#proof[For a prime $p$, the condition $v_(p)(n)>=k$ is equivalent to $n=p^k m$ with $m$ any positive integer. Summation gives probability $p^(-k sigma)$. For a finite list, simultaneous lower bounds replace $p^k$ by their product. Unique factorization and the same computation give the product of the marginal probabilities. Taking successive differences yields the joint mass at exponents $k_p>=0$ as
$ product_p (1-p^(-sigma))p^(-k_p sigma), $
where the product is over the list. So these exponents have independent geometric distributions starting at zero. Put $r=p^(-sigma)$. Differentiating the geometric series gives the mean $r/(1-r)$ and variance $r/(1-r)^2$ of one exponent.

Now $log n=sum_p v_(p)(n)log p$ is a finite sum for each outcome, although the list of possible primes is unbounded. Truncate at $P$ and use independence. The expected truncated logarithm is
$ sum_(p<=P) frac(log p,p^sigma-1). $
The random sums increase to $log n$, so their expectations converge by monotone convergence. For the variances use convergence in mean square. The pointwise difference tends to zero and its square is bounded by $(log n)^2$, which is integrable since $sum_n n^(-sigma)(log n)^2$ converges. The limiting variance is therefore
$ sum_p frac(p^(sigma)(log p)^2,(p^sigma-1)^2). $
The mean is $-frac(zeta'(sigma),zeta(sigma))$ and the variance is the second derivative of $log zeta(sigma)$. The latter also follows by differentiating the quotient for the mean with its minus sign. The probability calculation gives the prime-by-prime source of the nonnegative terms in that derivative.]

#example[Problem 9. What three point counts reveal][An elliptic curve over $𝔽_5$ has five rational points. Use the curve zeta theorem to predict its counts over the next two extension fields, and the number of closed points of degrees two and three. Why do these calculations involve divisibility by the extension degrees?]
#proof[Write $alpha, beta$ for the inverse roots. Then $alpha+beta=5+1-5=1$ and $alpha beta=5$. Their power sums $s_r$ obey $s_0=2$, $s_1=1$, and $s_r=s_(r-1)-5s_(r-2)$. Thus $s_2=-9$ and $s_3=-14$. From the logarithm of the zeta function,
$ N_2=25+1-(-9)=35, quad N_3=125+1-(-14)=140. $
A point defined first over degree $d$ has a Frobenius orbit of size $d$. All points in this orbit become rational over the extension of degree $r$ if and only if $d$ divides $r$. Therefore $N_r=sum_(d divides r)d B_d$ with $B_d$ the number of degree-$d$ closed points. Here $B_2=(35-5)/2=15$ and $B_3=(140-5)/3=45$. The divisions count whole orbits. As a consistency check the roots of $T^2-T+5$ have modulus $sqrt(5)$, giving $abs(s_r)<=2 dot 5^(r/2)$ for each $r$. This last conclusion follows from a statement about two numbers, while a collection of finitely many point counts alone would impose only finitely many tests.]

#example[Problem 10. Attenuating a zero term with a triangular average][For $h>0$ set $K_(h)(v)=max(h-abs(v),0)/h^2$. Given $f(u)=exp(beta u)cos(gamma u)$ on the real line, compute $integral_(-h)^h K_(h)(v)e^(-beta v) f(u+v)dif v$. Find all nonzero frequencies for which the result vanishes. Explain the role of the compensating exponential.]
#proof[Let $D_h$ be $1/h$ on $[-h/2,h/2]$ and zero elsewhere. The overlap of that interval with its translate by $v$ has length $max(h-abs(v),0)$. Consequently $D_h*D_h=K_h$, with $*$ denoting additive convolution on the logarithmic axis. Both functions have integral one. For the exponential $exp(i gamma v)$ the multiplier of $D_h$ is
$ frac(1,h) integral_(-h/2)^(h/2) exp(i gamma v)dif v
       =frac(sin(gamma h/2),gamma h/2). $
At zero its value by continuity is one. The integrals are over compact intervals, so Fubini gives the square of this multiplier for $K_h$. Multiplying $f(u+v)$ by $e^(-beta v)$ leaves the amplitude $e^(beta u)$ independent of $v$. Taking the real part proves that the answer is $f(u)$ times the square of the displayed sine quotient. It vanishes identically for $gamma=2pi k/h$ with $k$ a nonzero integer. Without compensation, the multiplier is evaluated at the complex exponent $beta+i gamma$; in general it no longer vanishes at those real frequencies. A smooth count can conceal a particular frequency even when its pointwise oscillation is large. The pole-detection argument in Chapter 9 uses localization and a movable transform center to retain the needed information.]

#example[Problem 11. Two independent signs modulo eight][On odd integers let $eta(n)=(-1)^((n-1)/2)$ and $nu(n)=(-1)^((n^2-1)/8)$. Express the indicator of the class $3$ modulo eight in their products. Apply the result to $Lambda(n)$ and compare the residue classes three and seven. Extend the formula correctly to all integers.]
#proof[The values of $(eta,nu)$ on $1,3,5,7$ are respectively $(1,1),(-1,-1),(1,-1),(-1,1)$. Thus on units the indicator for three is $(1-eta)(1-nu)/4$. Multiplicativity of each sign can be read from these two columns, since the unit group is generated by the commuting involutions three and five. Let $chi_0$ be the principal character modulo eight and extend the three nonprincipal characters by zero on even integers. Expanding the polynomial as an identity of extended characters gives
$ 4 bold(1)_(n equiv 3 " mod " 8)=chi_(0)(n)-eta(n)-nu(n)+(eta nu)(n). $
Keeping the constant function one in place of $chi_0$ would make the identity false at every even integer. Multiplication by $Lambda(n)$ followed by summation to $x$ gives the corresponding formula in the four character sums. For seven, the polynomial on units is $(1-eta)(1+nu)/4$. Subtraction shows that twice the difference of the two class sums equals the character sum for $eta nu$ minus the one for $nu$. In particular the principal character and the character induced from modulus four both disappear. This finite calculation separates a statement about a conductor from one about a modulus in which the sum was originally written.]

#example[Problem 12. A restriction map on actual cocycles][Let a topological generator $g$ of $G=ℤ_5$ act on the additive group $A=ℤ slash 125ℤ$ by multiplication by six. Compute $H^(1)(G,A)$, the same group for the closed subgroup generated by $g^5$, and the kernel and image of restriction. Use continuous cocycles throughout.]
#proof[For a putative cocycle write $b=z(g)$. Iterating the cocycle relation gives
$ z(g^n)=(1+6+dots+6^(n-1))b=frac(6^n-1,5)b $
for positive $n$. The binomial theorem gives $v_(5)(6^(5^k)-1)=k+1$ for $k>=0$. Indeed the first raising to the fifth power adds one to $v_(5)(6-1)$, and if $v_(5)(y-1)>=1$ the linear term in $(1+(y-1))^5-1$ has strictly smaller valuation than its four higher-degree terms. At $n=125$ both the action of $g^n$ and the cocycle sum are trivial modulo 125. Every $b$ therefore defines a cocycle on a finite quotient, hence a continuous cocycle on $G$. Conversely the value at a generator determines the cocycle on a dense subgroup and then everywhere by continuity. The coboundaries have $b=(6-1)a=5a$. This gives $H^(1)(G,A)=A slash (5A)$, of order five.

For the subgroup, its generator acts by $6^5$. The same reasoning gives the group $A slash ((6^5-1)A)=A slash (25A)$, of order 25. The restricted class has generator value $((6^5-1)/5)b=1555b$. Reduction modulo 25 gives $5b$. The image is thus the subgroup of multiples of five modulo 25, and the kernel is zero. A weaker calculation comparing only the two group orders could at most bound the size of the kernel. The generator calculation determines the map.]

#example[Problem 13. A rank-two index in three-dimensional space][Give $ℤ^3$ its Euclidean metric. Let $L$ have basis $(2,0,0),(1,3,3)$. Compute its area, the covolume of its saturation in its real span, and its index in that saturation. Determine which of these is detected by the greatest common divisor of the maximal minors.]
#proof[The rational plane is $y=z$. Intersecting it with $ℤ^3$ gives the saturated lattice $P$ with orthogonal basis $e_1,(0,1,1)$. Its covolume in the plane is $sqrt(2)$. In this basis the columns defining $L$ have coordinate matrix with rows $(2,1)$ and $(0,3)$, so the lattice index is six. The Gram matrix for the original columns has diagonal entries $4,19$ and off-diagonal entry two. Its determinant is $76-4=72$, giving Euclidean area $6sqrt(2)$. This agrees with index times saturated covolume.

The three maximal minors in ambient coordinates are $6,6,0$, with greatest common divisor six. Equivalently their signed list is the cross product, a multiple of the primitive integer normal $(0,-1,1)$. Taking the greatest common divisor extracts the integer multiplier. Taking the Euclidean norm retains the length of the primitive normal as well. A congruence calculation measures an index, whereas an archimedean norm uses a metric. Their comparison takes the shape just verified even in this small example.]

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[The analytic test and its arithmetic realization]

#example[Problem 14. Recover the coefficients from a continuous count][For bounded coefficients $a_n$ with $a_1=0$, set $S(x)=sum_(n<=x)a_(n)(x/n-1)$. Suppose the function is known for every positive real $x$. Recover every $a_n$ using only its values at three neighboring integers. Does subtracting a linear center affect the answer?]
#proof[Each individual ramp has constant slope $0$ to the left of $n$ and $1/n$ to its right. On an interval $[m,m+1]$ the full sum is linear, including the endpoints by continuity. Therefore its right slope at $m$ is $S(m+1)-S(m)$ and its left slope is $S(m)-S(m-1)$. Their difference is exactly $a_m/m$, because every other ramp is differentiable across $m$. Thus for all $m>=2$,
$ a_m=m(S(m+1)-2S(m)+S(m-1)). $
Any affine function has zero second difference. Consequently the centering used for a nonprincipal character preserves this recovery formula. The principal center contains $x log x$, whose second difference does not vanish. For it the correction can be computed before applying the formula. Indeed
$ (m+1)log(m+1)-2m log m+(m-1)log(m-1)
       =integral_(-1)^1 frac(1-abs(v),m+v) dif v, $
by two applications of the fundamental theorem of calculus. Multiplication by $m$ makes this correction of order one at large $m$. Dropping it would systematically change the reconstructed von Mangoldt coefficients. This example describes exactly how the original prime data survives a continuous interpolation.]

#example[Problem 15. A finite difference that kills both centers][For a function $S$ on positive reals put $D S(x)=S(x)-4S(x/2)+4S(x/4)$. Show that $D$ annihilates $x$ and $x log x$. Compute its Mellin multiplier for functions extended by zero below one. Can its sign pattern be used directly in the one-sided arithmetic upper bound?]
#proof[On $S(x)=x^s$ the operation acts by multiplication by $1-4 dot 2^(-s)+4 dot 4^(-s)=(1-2^(1-s))^2$. The multiplier and its derivative vanish at $s=1$. Substituting $s=1$, then differentiating the identity in $s$, proves both cancellation claims for these functions on the positive axis. The statements also hold at $x>=4$ if these functions are zero-extended below one. For a general zero-extended $S$, change variables $x=a y$ in the integral of $S(x/a)x^(-s-1)$ over positive $x$. It is $a^(-s)$ times the transform of $S$. The same multiplier results, initially on a half-plane of absolute convergence. Its zeros all have real part one. A transform pole in the open critical strip therefore survives.

The arithmetic input is a bound above for the original real observable on a good interval. An upper bound on $S(x/2)$ gives a *lower* bound on $-4S(x/2)$. Without a lower bound it cannot yield an upper bound for the new combination, even when all three arguments lie in the good range. For example at a chosen scale assign values $0,-M,0$ to the three terms before multiplication, with $M>0$. An upper bound zero holds separately, while their weighted combination is $4M$. Transform manipulations that preserve the pole need not preserve the inequality available to detect it.]

#example[Problem 16. How much probability remains in a fixed relative window?][Let $X$ have density $x^(k-1)e^(-x)/(k-1)!$ for $x>0$, with $k$ a positive integer. Give exponential bounds for the two events $X>1.2k$ and $X<0.8k$ using a freely chosen exponential moment. Compare with the estimate obtained from variance alone.]
#proof[Direct integration gives $bold(E)e^(t X)=(1-t)^(-k)$ for $t<1$. For $a>1$ and $0<t<1$, the upper-tail probability at $a k$ is at most $exp(-t a k)(1-t)^(-k)$. For $0<a<1$ the same expression with $t<0$ bounds the lower-tail probability, since $e^(t X)$ is then decreasing. Differentiating the logarithm in $t$ at fixed $a$ gives the optimum $t=1-1/a$. Substituting yields
$ exp(-k(a-1-log a)). $
The rate is positive for $a!=1$ by strict concavity of $log$. At $a=1.2$ the value of the rate is about $0.01768$ and at $a=0.8$ it is about $0.02314$. The probability of lying outside the open relative window is bounded by the sum of the two exponentials. On the other hand $bold(E)X=k$ and the variance is $k$, so Chebyshev's inequality gives only $25/k$. Both prove concentration. Only an exponential tail with a specified rate can absorb the exponential factors that occur when comparing normalized derivatives with a pole contribution. The scaled kernels in Chapter 9 have precisely this gamma density after normalization.]

#example[Problem 17. Which coefficients are probabilities?][On $C=ℤ slash 5ℤ$ prescribe the Fourier coefficients of a symmetric probability measure. Let the trivial coefficient equal one, the two characters of frequencies $1,-1$ have coefficient $a>=0$, and the two of frequencies $2,-2$ have coefficient zero. For which $a$ does the measure exist? Would it detect poles attached only to frequency two?]
#proof[The Fourier inversion formula determines the mass uniquely as
$ P(j)=frac(1+2a cos(2pi j/5),5), quad j=0,1,2,3,4. $
Its sum is one because the cosine sums to zero. Its least value for $a>=0$ occurs at $j=2,3$, where the cosine is $-(1+sqrt(5))/4$. To obtain this value one can pair the inverse fifth roots of unity in their sum $1+z+dots+z^4=0$ and solve for $z+z^(-1)$. Nonnegativity at those two residues is equivalent to $a<=2/(1+sqrt(5))$. All masses at the other residues are then positive. Thus the interval including its endpoints gives all solutions. At its upper endpoint two masses vanish, but the frequency-one contribution persists.

A pole seen only by the frequency-two character and its conjugate would receive coefficient zero, whatever its multiplicity. If the purpose of the family is to test every power of a target, one must give each retained power a strictly positive coefficient. The nearly uniform cyclic distribution in Chapter 10 meets that stronger requirement. Finite positivity of the *masses* guarantees a probability measure. The values of its Fourier transform determine which characters a particular analytic argument actually tests.]

#example[Problem 18. A dependent entry in a cyclotomic field][Work in $K=ℚ(zeta_(16))$. Let the first test on a unit be its image modulo $plus.minus 1$. Add the quadratic character of conductor eight given by $nu(n)=(-1)^((n^2-1)/8)$. Compute the fixed field for the joint kernel. What happens on adding also the character of conductor four?]
#proof[The first test is the quotient of the order-eight unit group by the involution $n mapsto -n$. Its kernel is the pair of classes $1,-1$. Under the cyclotomic isomorphism the element $-1$ acts by complex conjugation. The corresponding fixed field is the degree-four real subfield generated by $zeta_(16)+zeta_(16)^(-1)$. The second test has $nu(-1)=1$, since its defining exponent is zero there. Hence it is trivial on the kernel and factors through the first quotient. Its inclusion leaves both kernel and fixed field unchanged. One can see the quadratic subfield explicitly from $zeta_8+zeta_8^(-1)=sqrt(2)$ with $zeta_8=zeta_(16)^2$.

The character of conductor four takes $-1$ to $-1$. It removes the nonidentity member of the two-element kernel. The fixed field is then all of $K$. Multiplying the three target orders would suggest $4 dot 2 dot 2=16$, larger even than the full degree. It is the order of the joint image that governs the calculation. This is the finite-field-degree issue that requires care when the field tests of Chapter 11 involve a power of an already present character.]

#example[Problem 19. Following the measures through an adjoint][Let two finite sets have two points each. Give the source masses $1/2,1/3$ and the target masses $1/3,1/2$. A kernel, with rows indexed by the target, has rows $(2,3)$ and $(0,1)$. Form the integral operator with the source masses and its adjoint with the target masses. Verify the period identity on the source vector $(1,3)$ and target vector $(3,2)$.]
#proof[Let $M_X,M_Y$ be the two diagonal mass matrices and $K$ the displayed kernel matrix. For column vectors the operator is $T=K M_X$. Compute the pairing with a target vector using $M_Y$. Moving $K$ to the other side of the finite double sum shows $T^*=K^t M_Y$ for the two weighted scalar products. In rows these two operators are respectively $(1,1),(0,1/3)$ and $(2/3,0),(1,1/2)$. The weights have already been inserted exactly once. The ordinary transpose of the first operator would implement the adjoint for counting measure.

For the given vectors $T(1,3)=(4,1)$ and $T^(*)(3,2)=(2,4)$. Their pairings are $4 dot 3/3+1 dot 2/2=5$ and $1 dot 2/2+3 dot 4/3=5$. Multiplying both rational operators by six yields integer matrices and scales both pairings by six. This last statement uses a common normalization. Clearing a different denominator on each side without recording the ratio would alter the asserted identity. The arithmetic period construction has an analogous normalization issue once rational periods enter integral lattices.]

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[Divisibility, cohomology, and the final estimate]


#example[Problem 20. An exact factorial allowance][Let $p$ be prime, let $d>=1$, and put $q=p^d$. A family of two-variable derivatives at the origin of total order $q$ has the possible integral factors $k!(q-k)!$ for all $1<=k<q$. Determine the largest power of $p$ dividing every such factor. How does the answer change if both orders must be multiples of $p^a$ for a fixed $0<=a<d$? Work out the answers when $q=27$ and $a=1$. Explain when they give a sharp uniform lower valuation for sums of the derivatives.]

#proof[Counting positive multiples of $p^i$ gives
$ v_(p)(q!)=p^(d-1)+dots+p+1=frac(q-1,p-1). $
To distribute this valuation between $k!$ and $(q-k)!$, use
$ binom(q,k)=frac(q,k)binom(q-1,k-1). $
Every coefficient of $(1+z)^(q-1)$ is a unit modulo $p$. Indeed $(1+z)^q=1+z^q$ in the polynomial ring over $𝔽_p$, by iterating the Frobenius identity. Dividing by $1+z$ gives $1-z+z^2-dots+(-z)^(q-1)$, whose coefficients are all nonzero, also when $p=2$. It follows that $v_(p)(binom(q,k))=d-v_(p)(k)$. Thus
$ v_(p)(k!(q-k)!)=frac(q-1,p-1)-d+v_(p)(k). $
The smallest valuation is $(q-1)/(p-1)-d$, attained already at $k=1$. If $p^a$ divides $k$, it also divides $q-k$. The least allowed valuation rises by $a$ and is attained at $k=p^a$. Exponentiating these two minima with base $p$ gives the requested greatest common powers.

For $q=27$ one has $p=3,d=3$ and $v_(3)(27!)=13$. The first minimum is ten. When both orders must be multiples of three it is eleven. Dividing the derivatives by $3^9$ therefore leaves at least one power of three in the unrestricted family and at least two in the restricted family. Every integral linear combination inherits the lower valuation by the ultrametric inequality. If the common exponent is $v$, equality for a particular sum holds exactly when its reduction after division by $p^v$ is nonzero. Only minimizing profiles can contribute to that reduction, and their unit residues can cancel. In the unrestricted polynomial model $X^k Y^(q-k)$ has exactly one nonzero derivative at the origin among the indicated profiles, with value $k!(q-k)!$. A minimizing $k$ therefore attains the uniform bound. Further relations in a particular geometric kernel could increase its valuation.]

#example[Problem 21. Two boundary regimes of the slope loss][After setting aside the word-count term, divide the loss in the slope inequality by $H>0$. Its value is
$ L=frac(4u log(1+1/u)+eta+4sqrt(e),1-delta), quad u=U/H. $
Assume $x>=2$ and take the exact scaling $u=x^(-a)$, $eta=x^(-b)$, $e=x^(-2b)$, and $1-delta=x^(-g)$, with $a,b>0$ and $g>=0$. Characterize when $L$ stays bounded and when $L$ tends to zero. Compare the cases $(a,b,g)=(1/2,1/3,1/3)$ and $(1/3,1/2,1/3)$. If only $0<=u<=x^(-a)$ is known, are the conditions you found sufficient? Extend $u log(1+1/u)$ continuously at zero for this last question.]

#proof[For the displayed choices the expression is exactly
$ L=4x^(g-a) log(1+x^a)+5x^(g-b). $
The five consists of one unit for the overlap and four for the square-root error. The logarithm has the decomposition $log(1+x^a)=a log x+log(1+x^(-a))$. Its second summand is between zero and $x^(-a)$, by $log(1+t)<=t$. Thus the first term of $L$ tends to zero if $g<a$, grows logarithmically if $g=a$, and grows faster if $g>a$. The second tends to zero if $g<b$, equals five if $g=b$, and is unbounded if $g>b$. Both are nonnegative. It follows that boundedness is equivalent to $g<a$ and $g<=b$, while convergence to zero is equivalent to $g<min(a,b)$.

For the first triple the logarithmic term tends to zero since $g-a=-1/6$, and $L$ tends to five. For the second triple the algebraic term tends to zero, but the other is $4log(1+x^(1/3))$ and diverges. Exchanging the roles of the two small losses changes the answer at exactly the exponent where division by the surviving rank fraction cancels their small size.

For the upper-bound variant, the function $h(u)=u log(1+1/u)$ increases on $u>0$. Its derivative is $log(1+s)-s/(1+s)$ with $s=1/u$. This last expression vanishes at $s=0$ and has derivative $s/(1+s)^2>=0$. Also $h(u)$ tends to zero as $u$ decreases to zero. Therefore its value anywhere in the permitted interval is bounded by its value at $x^(-a)$. The stated sufficient conditions continue to hold, and the endpoint choice shows their sharpness as uniform conditions over all permitted $u$. Necessity for a particular lattice would require a lower bound on its actual spread. These distinctions matter when the geometry supplies only upper bounds for overlap and dispersion.]

#example[Problem 22. Local restrictions at different depths][Take the additive profinite group $G=ℤ_ℓ^2$ and give $M=ℤ slash ℓ^r ℤ$ the trivial action, with $r>=1$. Restrict to the closed subgroups generated by $(1,0)$ and $(1,ℓ^k)$ for an integer $k>=0$. Their residual lines agree for $k>=1$ and are distinct for $k=0$. Compute the length over $ℤ_ℓ$ of the kernel and the cokernel of the joint restriction on continuous $H^1$. Add the subgroup generated by $(0,ℓ^h)$ for an integer $h>=0$ and determine how the kernel changes. At $ℓ=5$, $r=4$, compare $k=1$ and $k=3$ before the third restriction.]

#proof[With trivial action the continuous one-cocycles are exactly the continuous homomorphisms, and the coboundaries vanish. Such a homomorphism from $ℤ_ℓ^2$ is determined by its values $A,B in M$ on the two standard generators. Conversely arbitrary $A,B$ define a continuous homomorphism by reducing the two arguments modulo $ℓ^r$. Each closed procyclic subgroup in the problem is isomorphic to $ℤ_ℓ$ through its indicated generator, so evaluation at that generator identifies its $H^1$ with $M$.

The joint restriction sends $(A,B)$ to $(A,A+ℓ^k B)$. Subtract the first output from the second. This invertible row operation identifies the map with the diagonal map $(A,B) mapsto (A,ℓ^k B)$. The kernel is the annihilator in $M$ of multiplication by $ℓ^k$, of order $ℓ^(min(k,r))$. The cokernel is $M slash ℓ^k M$, of the same order. Both lengths are $min(k,r)$. For $k=0$ the joint map is an isomorphism, and for $k>=r$ the second congruence adds nothing once $A=0$ is imposed.

Adding the third subgroup requires $ℓ^h B=0$ as well. The two annihilators are nested in the cyclic module $M$, so the new kernel length is $min(k,h,r)$. A third restriction can leave the answer unchanged even though it is imposed at a distinct subgroup. In the suggested numerical cases the first pair of generators spans the same *line modulo five* in both cases. At modulus $5^4$ the two kernel orders are nevertheless $5$ and $125$. This is a finite model for an integral issue in localization. Computing the rank of a residual map can fail to determine how much logarithmic cardinality its restriction kernel contributes. At a coefficient prime, it is the full length multiplied by $log ℓ$ that enters the comparison.]


#example[Problem 23. The sharp window for one obstruction][Let $B>1$, $lambda>1$, and $U>0$. An exceptional point $a>=0$ forbids $u in [a,B a)$ on the logarithmic scale. Find the largest constant $c=c(B,lambda)$ such that for *every* $a$ there is a closed interval $[y,lambda y]$ with $y>=c U$, $lambda y<=U$, which avoids all forbidden points. In particular justify the endpoints in the equality case, prove optimality, and discuss what changes at $B=1$.]

#proof[The sharp answer is $c=1/(B lambda^2)$. To obtain the interval, compare $a$ with $a_0=U/(B lambda)$. If $a<=a_0$, let $y=U/lambda$. Then $B a<=y$ and the entire chosen interval is to the right of the forbidden interval. This includes $a=a_0$, for which $y=B a$ is the first permitted point at that end. If $a>a_0$, take $y=U/(B lambda^2)$. Its upper endpoint is then $a_0<a$, so this interval lies strictly to the left of the forbidden one. In both cases the interval stays within the budget, and its left endpoint is at least the advertised multiple of $U$. The case $a=0$ also has an empty forbidden interval and is covered by the first choice.

For sharpness take $c'>1/(B lambda^2)$. We may choose $a$ strictly between $a_0$ and $lambda c' U$, since that upper endpoint is strictly larger than $a_0$. For $a>0$ the forbidden interval is nonempty. An interval avoiding it must satisfy either $lambda y<a$ or $y>=B a$. A right-hand choice would require $y>=B a>U/lambda$, violating its upper budget. For a left-hand choice, $y<a/lambda<c' U$. Thus no larger constant works for all possible obstructions. This shows why moving $a$ just *above* $a_0$ matters. At equality there is a much larger permitted interval beginning exactly at the open right endpoint of the obstruction.

For $B=1$ the forbidden interval is empty for every $a$, and the largest constant is $1/lambda$. For $B>1$ and $lambda=2$ the sharp single-obstruction constant is $frac(1,4B)$. Chapter 16's general finite-set theorem, specialized to a cardinality bound of one, guarantees the smaller constant $frac(1,8B)$ and so is consistent with this refinement. For example with $B=3$ and $lambda=3/2$ the sharp result ensures $y>=4U/27$. Writing $U=log X$ gives an interval $[Y,Y^(3/2)]$ with $Y>=X^(4/27)$. The optimization here is over all positions of one real obstruction. A particular set of integer twists can permit a wider choice.]

#example[Problem 24. Optimizing the four costs with sparse sets][In a numerical model with $x>1$, suppress the controlled $x^epsilon$ factors. Take $a_E=x b$, $n=x^nu$, $n_T/n=x^(-alpha)$, and $n_2/n=x^(-beta)$ as positive real quantities with $alpha,beta,nu>=0$. Let $m=x^r$ and $k=x^l$ with $r>=0$ and $l$ real. Use the four rows of Chapter 17, including the final division by $delta_2=n_2/n$, to minimize the largest resulting power of $x$. For $alpha=1/10$ and $beta=1/20$ solve the problem for $nu=3/10$ and $nu=3/20$. For actual nonempty finite sets, their sizes at least one would impose the additional restrictions $alpha<=nu$ and $beta<=nu$.]

#proof[Before division by the thick density, the four exponents are
$ 1-r, quad 1+r-l, quad r+alpha, quad l-2nu+alpha-beta. $
Division by $delta_2$ adds $beta$ to their largest exponent. For fixed $r$, choose $l=frac(1+r+2nu-alpha+beta,2)$. The second and fourth expressions are then equal to their mean $frac(1+r-2nu+alpha-beta,2)$, the smallest achievable value of their maximum. If all four expressions are at most $M$, then $M>=alpha$ since $r>=0$. Adding the first and third gives $2M>=1+alpha$. Adding the first, second and fourth gives $3M>=2-2nu+alpha-beta$. Thus the optimum before division is at least
$ M_0=max(alpha,frac(1+alpha,2),frac(2-2nu+alpha-beta,3)). $
Choose $r=max(0,1-M_0)$ and choose $l$ as above. When $M_0<=1$, substitution bounds the third term by $M_0$ because $2M_0>=1+alpha$, and the common second and fourth term by $M_0$ because $3M_0>=2-2nu+alpha-beta$. The first term equals $M_0$. When $M_0>=1$, take $r=0$. The first and third terms are at most $M_0$, and the common value of the others is at most $frac(1+alpha,2)<=M_0$ by nonnegativity of $nu,beta$. Hence $beta+M_0$ is exactly the desired exponent over the whole stated parameter range.

For both numerical examples the first and third terms meet at the feasible $r_0=9/20$, with value $11/20$. When $nu=3/10$, the second and fourth exponents at $r_0$ can both be made at most $11/20$. The corresponding conditions on $l$ are $9/10<=l<=11/10$. Take $l=1$. This attains the lower bound, and the optimized exponent after division is $11/20+1/20=3/5$.

For $nu=3/20$ the two $l$-dependent terms cannot be made so small at $r_0$. The mean lower bound for them at an arbitrary $r$ is $(r+3/4)/2$. The maximum of this increasing function and $1-r$ is at least their intersection value. They meet at $r=5/12$, where the value is $7/12$. The remaining $r+alpha=31/60$ lies below $7/12$. Choosing $l=5/6$ makes the second and fourth exponents both equal to $7/12$, so the new lower bound is attained. The final exponent is $7/12+1/20=19/30$.

The shared density expense $beta$ is identical in the two examples. The extra cost in the second case arises because shrinking the ambient host limits the range of $k$ for which the second and fourth rows can both be small. The model distinguishes that constraint from the familiar balance between the first and third rows. It optimizes continuous comparison data. A number-field construction has to supply integral ranks and all its additional hypotheses before such a numerical estimate can be applied.]

#example[Problem 25. Boundary zeros introduced by changing a modulus][Let $chi$ be the nonprincipal primitive character modulo three and let $chi'$ be its induction to modulus $42$. Locate all zeros of the two Euler factors that distinguish $L(s,chi')$ from $L(s,chi)$, including their multiplicities. Show that the two sets are disjoint. Compute $L'(0,chi')$, where the prime on $L$ means differentiation with respect to $s$. You may use $zeta(0,a)=1/2-a$ for the Hurwitz zeta function and classical nonvanishing on the line of real part one.]

#proof[The additional primes dividing $42$ are two and seven. Their primitive character values are $chi(2)=-1$ and $chi(7)=1$. Removing their Euler factors in the half-plane of absolute convergence, and then continuing, gives
$ L(s,chi')=L(s,chi)(1+2^(-s))(1-7^(-s)). $
The zeros of the first extra factor are $s=frac((2k+1)pi i,log 2)$ for integers $k$. The zeros of the second are $s=frac(2j pi i,log 7)$ for integers $j$. At any zero its factor has derivative respectively $log 2$ or $log 7$, obtained by substituting $2^(-s)=-1$ or $7^(-s)=1$ in the derivative. Each zero is simple in its corresponding factor. Coincidence of two locations would yield $(2k+1)log 7=2j log 2$ and hence $7^(2k+1)=2^(2j)$. Taking the valuation at seven on both sides rules this out, since the exponent $2k+1$ is never zero.

At zero the second factor vanishes and the first equals two. Separating the two nonzero residue classes modulo three in the Dirichlet series gives
$ L(s,chi)=3^(-s)(zeta(s,1/3)-zeta(s,2/3)). $
The supplied special value now gives $L(0,chi)=1/3$. Differentiation of the product shows $L'(0,chi')=(2/3)log 7$, since the term differentiating the vanishing factor is the only survivor. At any of the listed nonzero imaginary points the primitive function is nonzero as well. The functional equation for the odd real character relates its completed value there to its value at the reflected point of real part one, where the primitive function is nonzero. The gamma factors have finite nonzero values at both points. Thus each of the boundary zeros computed here has multiplicity one in the induced function itself.

For $Re s>0$ both $abs(2^(-s))$ and $abs(7^(-s))$ are strictly smaller than one. The two factors have no zeros there. This explicit example reconciles the equality of primitive and induced zero sets in the open critical strip with their different behavior on its left boundary. A statement about an entire closed half-plane would have to retain the deleted Euler factors.]




